linktest-os — READ ME FIRST
============================
Instructions for downloading, flashing, and booting linktest-os on two
laptops, written for someone who hasn't done this before. Uses a Windows 11
PC to do the downloading and USB-flashing.

BEFORE YOU START: PROJECT STATUS
---------------------------------
This project is still in active development (see the main README.md,
"Status" section). As of this writing there may not be a finished ISO
published yet on the GitHub Releases page. If you don't see one when you
get to Step 2 below, stop and check with whoever gave you this drive/laptop
setup, or ask in the project's GitHub Discussions — don't assume it's
your fault. The rest of this guide will apply exactly as written the
moment a release is published.

WHAT YOU'LL NEED
-----------------
  - 2 USB flash drives, 4 GB or bigger (they will be COMPLETELY ERASED)
  - 2 laptops (or PCs), each with an Ethernet port, or a USB-to-Ethernet
    adapter if the laptop doesn't have one built in
  - 1 standard Ethernet cable (any cable works — it does not need to be a
    special "crossover" cable)
  - A Windows 11 PC with internet access to do the downloading and
    flashing (this can be one of the two test laptops, or a third machine)

Nothing gets installed on either laptop's hard drive. linktest-os runs
entirely from the USB stick and from RAM — when you're done, you remove
the USB drive and the laptop boots normally again, untouched.


STEP 1 — Download a flashing tool: Rufus
------------------------------------------
On the Windows 11 PC, go to:

    https://rufus.ie

and download Rufus (the "portable" version needs no installation — just
save the .exe file somewhere you can find it, like your Downloads folder
or Desktop).

(If you already prefer another tool like balenaEtcher, that works too —
just skip to Step 3 and use its equivalent buttons.)


STEP 2 — Download the linktest-os ISO
----------------------------------------
Go to:

    https://github.com/aecaola/linktest-os/releases

and download the latest linktest-os.iso file (it's the actual "distro"
you're going to put on the USB drives — a few hundred MB). If a
.iso.sha256 file is also listed, you can download that too, but it's
optional — it's just a way to double check the download wasn't corrupted.

There is only ONE iso file — both USB drives get the exact same one.
There's no "drive A" vs "drive B."


STEP 3 — Flash the ISO onto BOTH USB drives
----------------------------------------------
Do this whole step twice — once per USB drive.

  1. Plug in a USB drive. WARNING: everything currently on it will be
     erased. Make sure it's empty or that you don't need anything on it.

  2. Double-click Rufus. If Windows asks "Do you want to allow this app
     to make changes to your device?", click Yes.

  3. In Rufus, under "Device", make sure the drive shown is the USB
     stick you just plugged in — not your Windows hard drive. If you
     have other drives plugged in, double- and triple-check this.

  4. Under "Boot selection", click SELECT (or the small disk icon) and
     choose the linktest-os.iso file you downloaded in Step 2.

  5. Leave the rest of the settings on their defaults. If Rufus pops up
     a dialog asking how to write the image (e.g. "ISO Image mode" vs
     "DD Image mode"), choose "DD Image mode" (sometimes just called
     "Write in DD mode") if you're given the choice — this writes the
     ISO exactly as-is, which is what a live/bootable Linux image needs.

  6. Click START. Confirm the "this will erase the drive" warning.

  7. Wait for the progress bar to reach "READY" (usually a couple of
     minutes). Don't unplug the drive while it's running.

  8. Eject the drive (Windows' "Safely Remove Hardware," or just wait
     until Rufus says it's done and unplug it).

  9. Repeat steps 1–8 with the second USB drive and the same ISO file.

You now have two identical bootable linktest-os USB drives.


STEP 4 — Turn off Secure Boot (one-time, per laptop)
-------------------------------------------------------
Most Windows 11 laptops ship with a security feature called "Secure Boot"
turned on, which blocks USB drives that aren't from Microsoft or a small
list of approved vendors. linktest-os isn't signed for Secure Boot, so it
needs to be turned off, or the laptop will refuse to boot from the USB
drive (it'll just boot into Windows as normal, or show an error).

You only need to do this once per laptop; it won't affect normal Windows
use afterward.

  1. Click the Windows Start menu, then the gear icon (Settings).
  2. Go to System > Recovery.
  3. Next to "Advanced startup", click Restart now.
  4. The PC restarts into a blue menu. Click Troubleshoot >
     Advanced options > UEFI Firmware Settings > Restart.
  5. The PC restarts again into its UEFI/BIOS setup screen (blue or gray,
     mouse-and-keyboard or keyboard-only, looks different on every brand).
     Look for a tab or menu called "Security" or "Boot" and find
     "Secure Boot". Set it to Disabled.
  6. Find "Save and Exit" (often the F10 key) and confirm.

Do this on BOTH laptops.


STEP 5 — Boot both laptops from USB
---------------------------------------
Do this on BOTH laptops, one at a time.

  1. Plug a flashed USB drive into the laptop.
  2. Restart the laptop.
  3. As it's restarting, you need to open a one-time "boot menu" instead
     of letting it boot into Windows normally. This is done by tapping a
     key repeatedly right after the screen goes dark/shows a logo. The
     key depends on the laptop brand:

         Dell ................ F12
         Lenovo .............. F12  (some: Novo button / F2)
         HP .................. F9   (some: Esc, then F9)
         ASUS ................ F8  or  Esc
         Acer ................ F12  (may need enabling in BIOS first)
         MSI ................. F11
         Surface / generic UEFI ... hold Volume Down while powering on,
                                      or use Step 4's "Advanced startup"
                                      route and choose "Use a device"
                                      instead of "UEFI Firmware Settings"

     If none of those work, use the same "Advanced startup" route as
     Step 4, but at the blue Troubleshoot menu choose "Use a device"
     instead of "UEFI Firmware Settings" — this always works regardless
     of brand.

  4. From the boot menu, select the USB drive. It may be listed by brand
     name (e.g. "SanDisk USB Device") or as "UEFI: <drive name>" — if
     both are listed, try the UEFI one first.

  5. The screen will show a lot of scrolling text for a few seconds —
     that's Linux booting, this is normal, you don't need to read it.

  6. It should land directly on the linktest-os menu screen — no login
     prompt, nothing to type. If you instead see Windows start up
     normally, the boot menu selection didn't take; restart and try
     again, or double-check Step 4 (Secure Boot).


STEP 6 — Connect the cable and run a test
----------------------------------------------
  1. With both laptops sitting at the linktest-os menu, plug the
     Ethernet cable into both laptops' Ethernet ports (built-in port, or
     a USB-Ethernet adapter — see docs/COMPATIBILITY.md in this repo if
     you're not sure yours is supported).
  2. Watch the "State" line at the top of the menu on both screens. It
     will move through a few stages on its own (this takes a few
     seconds) and should settle on READY, with a "Peer" address filled
     in on both screens.
  3. On EITHER laptop (doesn't matter which), select "Run Test" from the
     menu and pick a duration.
  4. After it finishes, you'll see a results screen: throughput, plus
     whether any errors/drops showed up on the link during the test.

When you're done, just power off the laptop (or remove the USB drive and
restart) — nothing was changed on the laptop itself.


IF SOMETHING'S NOT WORKING
-----------------------------
  - Stuck at "NO_LINK": check the cable is fully plugged in on both
    ends, and that you're using the Ethernet port (not a USB port) on
    both machines.
  - Stuck at "PEER_DISCOVERY" and never reaches READY: make sure BOTH
    laptops actually booted into linktest-os (not one of them still
    running Windows with a manually-configured IP) — the peer-discovery
    step needs both sides running it.
  - Using a USB-to-Ethernet dongle and nothing works: check
    docs/COMPATIBILITY.md — not every USB-C/USB-A Ethernet dongle chipset
    is supported yet, and that file tracks which ones are known-good.
  - Testing against a machine that ISN'T running linktest-os: use the
    menu's Advanced option to force server-only or client-only mode
    instead of waiting for normal peer discovery, which requires both
    sides.

To report a bug or ask a question, use Issues or Discussions on the
GitHub repo: https://github.com/aecaola/linktest-os
